# Tasks — example configuration. Copy to `.env` and edit.
#
# Every line is commented out and shows the default, so an unedited copy
# changes nothing. These are the six variables a person actually has to decide
# about; the complete table, with the reasoning attached, is in CLAUDE.md.
#
# Load order: `<data dir>/.env`, then the nearest `.env` at or above the
# working directory, then the nearest one at or above the binary. First to
# define a variable wins, and the real environment outranks all three.


# ---- Credentials ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Credentials do not belong in this file. They belong in the sealed store,
# which is where the server looks first:
#
#     tasks secrets init                    # unseal key -> Keychain
#                                           #   (or `--key-file PATH`)
#     tasks secrets set github-token        # value on stdin, never argv
#     tasks secrets set anthropic-api-key
#
# A sealed key is picked up by a *running* server with no restart, and it never
# reaches a VM: agents redeem short-lived, repo-bound leases through the
# in-process broker. See docs/plans/2026-08-18-credential-custody.md.
#
# The two variables below still work, as *fallbacks* rather than as the path to
# take: the server warns at startup for each one it falls back to, naming the
# `tasks secrets set` that should have answered instead.

# GitHub. The token must cover the whole write surface in
# crates/tasks/src/github.rs — create / merge / close a pull request,
# create / close / reopen / update an issue, set labels, issue and review
# comments, and the branch push behind a build. Classic token: `repo`.
# Fine-grained: Contents read+write, Pull requests read+write, Issues
# read+write, Metadata read. With no token at all, polling is disabled and the
# pipeline has no intake.
#GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_replace_me

# Anthropic. Resolution is: sealed store, then this variable, then the host's
# Claude Code apiKeyHelper at `~/.claude/anthropic_key.sh` (Anthropic only).
# The last step is worth stating because it is invisible — with the helper
# present, agent runs work with nothing set anywhere and nothing saying where
# the key came from, which is why it surprises the second machine.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-replace_me


# ---- The pipeline ---------------------------------------------------------

# The mode *every* boot starts in, overwriting whatever the last process left
# in the store: `pause`, `play` or `stop`. The default is the surprising part —
# a restart, a crash or a launchd relaunch always comes back quiet, intake and
# the API running and nothing dispatching. Say `play` on a host you want
# dispatching unattended. An unparseable value refuses to boot.
#TASKS_DEFAULT_MODE=pause

# Scouts at once. Each holds a vm-pool slot and the serial build lane holds one
# more, so the pool (6 by default) must fit this plus one — three is the
# recommended ceiling, and the server warns on every vm-pool connect when what
# it found is short or an exact fit. The tighter limit is memory, not slots: at
# the default VM shapes, three scouts plus a Builder plus the container
# runtime's buildkit reserve roughly 22 GB.
#SCOUT_MAX_CONCURRENT=2


# ---- The orchestrator -----------------------------------------------------
#
# What the orchestrator *may* do is the charter's business, not this file's;
# these two decide what it can reach. Both values contain spaces and so are
# quoted — `dotenvy` strips the quotes, and a parser that kept them would leave
# the program name starting with a double quote.
#
# (A) Curl-only, the default, and the shape the workdir default belongs to: a
#     scratch directory with no checkout in it, so there is nothing to edit.
#     `--allowedTools` is default-deny and prefix-matched, so an allowlist is
#     written in verbs. Do not extend it with an entry containing a space —
#     `Bash(git log:*)` is shattered into two permissions matching nothing by
#     the whitespace split in crates/tasks/src/orchestrator.rs. Leave the
#     workdir variable unset.
#ORCHESTRATOR_CMD="claude --print --output-format stream-json --verbose --include-partial-messages --allowedTools Bash(curl:*)"
#
# (B) A full dev agent pointed at this checkout. `--dangerously-skip-permissions`
#     *discards* the allowlist rather than widening it, so this shape is only
#     for a working directory you are content to have it edit. What it buys is
#     verification: with a checkout and a warm build directory
#     (ORCHESTRATOR_TARGET_DIR, see CLAUDE.md) the orchestrator can run the
#     tests behind a composition of open pull requests before landing it,
#     rather than handing every one of them back to a human.
#ORCHESTRATOR_CMD="claude --print --output-format stream-json --verbose --include-partial-messages --dangerously-skip-permissions"
#ORCHESTRATOR_WORKDIR=/absolute/path/to/this/checkout
